// An object is permuted while a comment is still pending further down the document.
//
// This shape is why the permuted separator's assert can only claim that a pending comment starts at or
// beyond the end of the object being printed. It cannot claim the gap it is handed is forward-ordered:
// the properties have moved, so the "previous" one can sit later in the source than the next, and a
// comment pass measuring that gap sees an inverted range.
//
// The YAML backend had exactly this assert too strong -- demanding either a forward gap or no pending
// comment at all -- and it crashed debug builds on an ordinary document while release was correct. The
// JSON backend carries the same assert, so it gets the same fixture rather than relying on the YAML one.
//
// `get` must come out reordered below, or this proves nothing: a refused object never reaches the
// permuted separator. It also pins the refusal as per-object, since the tail comment refuses the root
// (so `paths` stays before `info`) while `get` permutes regardless.
{
  "openapi": "3.0.0",
  "paths": { "/p": { "get": { "responses": {}, "summary": "s", "operationId": "op" } } },
  "info": { "version": "1", "title": "t" }
  // Still pending while `get` above is permuted, and belongs to nothing that moved.
}
